=7606ede1164df321a11fa37e03c1309bd8ec65a37d50c5dd220f3a65f910ab03&node=circuit.positive_finite_step&relation=direct","prerequisite_uses":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=7606ede1164df321a11fa37e03c1309bd8ec65a37d50c5dd220f3a65f910ab03&node=circuit.positive_finite_step&relation=prerequisites","prerequisites":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=7606ede1164df321a11fa37e03c1309bd8ec65a37d50c5dd220f3a65f910ab03&view=arguments&node=circuit.positive_finite_step&assumptions=true"},"neighborhood_href":"/api/v1/neighborhood?manifest=7606ede1164df321a11fa37e03c1309bd8ec65a37d50c5dd220f3a65f910ab03&ref=sha256%3A3592e6a1f4e5ac9cf05e86d1dac98fabd5ac5f25ba57c4ce6e79c2b2f110d0db%2Fnodes%2Fcircuit.positive_finite_step","record":{"assumption_ids":["circuit.positive_input"],"boundary":"Finite-degree successive messages are not independent. The exact EAMS Proposition 2.1 supplies the edge calculation; the QAOA source explicitly repairs the missing 1/k in EAMS display (2.21) and the shifted finite-sum endpoints in (4.5)/(4.18). The finite-step theorem is used separately from EAMS’s global optimization theorem.","citation_ids":["circuit-r2-26-cite-positive-finite-step","eams2023_finite_step"],"evidence_ids":[],"id":"circuit.positive_finite_step","kind":"claim","label":"EAMS finite-step law and corrected edge identity","locator":"07-positive-temperature.tex:211–450; lem:eams-finite-step, eq:eams-finite-normalization, eq:eams-corrected-value; EAMS arXiv:2111.06813v2 §§2–4.1","source_id":"circuit-r2-26-source-positive","statement":"Fix finite β₀,T,δ and L=floor(T/δ). The one-step-lag cavity recursion uses exact finite-degree normalizers c_{D,r}; node updates use the same normalizers. As D→∞, these constants converge, finite collections of cavity innovations converge jointly to independent standard Gaussians with all fixed moments, and z_L converges to Z_δ with E Z_δ²=Lδ. The scaled edge value equals in the limit δΣ_{r=1}^{L−1}c_{δ,r} E a(t_r,X_r^δ), with an empty sum when L=1.","status":"unverified"},"re